Terraform is an open-source infrastructure as code (IaC) tool that allows you to define and provision a datacenter infrastructure using a high-level configuration language known as HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL), or optionally JSON. It enables you to safely and predictably create, change, and improve infrastructure. It is an external tool to AWS and can be used to manage resources across multiple cloud providers, as well as on-premises and edge environments.

Here's a simple example of a Terraform configuration file that provisions an AWS EC2 instance:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "example-instance"
  }
}
```

This configuration specifies that an AWS provider should be used with the region set to `us-west-2`, and that an EC2 instance should be created with an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) ID of `ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0`, an instance type of `t2.micro`, and a tag with the key `Name` and the value `example-instance`.

